A||||2||ds21||Q. for Toddster or Turbo guys||||1||
Z||000000||ds21||10-02-2001||02:10 PM||||It was mentioned that a turbo system from a 90 Grand Prix would fit on a 2.8 I was wondering how much HP you can expect, and does anyone have any pics!<P>P.S. also if I can't find a Grand Prix, can you order the plumbing from G.M. How much? and what kind/size turbo would fit?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rick 88||10-03-2001||12:18 AM||||The 90 Grand Prix Turbo used a 3.1 V6 with a turbo and intercooler. It was rated at 205 horsepower with mild factory boost. Still not enough power for a Grand Prix but the best at the time. It would be difficult to package this set up in a Fiero because of the intercooler's location.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000002||00lE||10-03-2001||12:36 AM||||The components from the system may fit, but the crossover pipe is well over $1000 "new" from GM.  The plumbing could be done cheaper by a diy'er, since used TGP parts usually carry a premium price.  It is really the engine management system (code developed by McLaren) that actually sets the TGP apart from most "run of the mill" bolt on attempts.  The problem is that the TGP 3.1 uses DIS, so without changing out the 2.8 Fiero block (no crank sensor hole), its probably not going to work.  Swapping in the entire engine would be a possibilty though.<P>There are intercoolers small enough to fit below a Fiero engine compartment grate, and still efficient enough to compare with the TGP setup, if proper airflow diversion(side scoops) is addressed.<P>There are several aftermarket chips available for the TGP system that can  dramatically increase HP by enabling higher than stock boost levels.<P>The truely challenging issues with a TGP 3.1 swap are presented by the accessory mounting, dog bone, exhaust, and the PCM/engine wiring harness nightmare.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 10-03-2001).]||||reg||1
